    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently cataloging a series of ceramic shards from a Late Helladic II stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between different firing techniques used in this period. Could you explain how I can differentiate between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres based on the clay body and surface color?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oxidized firing, typically in an oxygen-rich atmosphere, usually results in warmer, reddish-brown tones in iron-bearing clays due to the presence of ferric oxide ($\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3$). Conversely, a reduced atmosphere starves the kiln of oxygen, causing iron oxides to convert to ferrous forms ($\\text{FeO}$), which often produces grays, blacks, or muted blues in the pottery. Examine the interior paste color alongside the surface glaze or slip finish for the clearest distinction between these firing states.",
      "source": "target",
